<spell-notebook "search usage">
	<header level="1">
		Search
		<header>A search module allows a user to query for results from a selection of data.</>
	</>
	<search-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#forms">Forms</>
			<item link="#security">Security</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (results, events, forms), keyboard, accessibility, framework snippets. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write `<ui-search>` and give it results. Name it with `aria-label` or a `<label for>`: the placeholder alone is
			not a name. Results come from one of two places:

			- the `source` PROPERTY, searched in the page. Plain HTML may write it as a JSON attribute (single-quoted), as
			  the examples do.
			  - `search-fields` picks the fields searched (`title description` by default).
			  - `full-text-search` says how loosely they match: a word START always matches; then `exact` anywhere (the
			    default), `fuzzy` in order, `prefix` nothing more, `some` any word, `all` every word.
			  - `ignore-diacritics` lets `a` match `á`; `max-results` caps the list (`0`: no limit).
			- or a server: `url` is a template with `{query}` (`/api/search?q={query}`). Typing waits `search-delay` ms, a
			  newer query cancels an older one, and answers are cached per query. The answer is Fomantic's shape:
			  `{ results: [...] }`, or `{ results: { key: { name, results: [...] } } }` for a `category` search.

			A result is Fomantic's `{ title, description, image, price, category, url }`. `min-characters` (default 1) is how
			much to type before a search runs.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const search = document.querySelector("ui-search")
			search.source = [
			  { title: "France", description: "EU", url: "/countries/fr" },
			  { title: "Ghana", description: "AU", url: "/countries/gh" }
			]
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-search` -- `{ query }`: the user typed a query at least `min-characters` long; it's about to run.
			- `ui-replied` -- `{ query, results }`: a remote query answered.
			- `ui-select` -- `{ result }`, cancelable: a result is about to be chosen (Fomantic's `onSelect`).
			  `preventDefault()` keeps the text, the results and the page.
			- `ui-changed` -- `{ value }`: the text was committed: a result chosen, the input cleared with Escape, or left
			  after an edit.
			- `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` -- `{ open }`, cancelable: the results are about to show / hide.

			Choosing a result fires `ui-select` first; then its title goes into the input (`ui-changed`), the results close,
			and a result with a `url` is followed.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			search.addEventListener("ui-select", (event) => {
			  event.preventDefault() // stay on the page
			  console.log(event.detail.result.title)
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms">
		<markdown>
			A search is a form-associated element, like Fomantic's real `<input class="prompt">`.

			- With a `name`, it submits the input's text; `value` is that text (the query, or a chosen result's title).
			- `required` means the input can't be empty.
			- `disabled` dims it and leaves it out of the form.
			- Enter with no result highlighted submits the form.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Security">
		<markdown>
			Fomantic's Security tab warns that results from a foreign API may carry HTML. Ours render every field as TEXT
			(`highlight-matches` wraps the query in a real `<mark>`, never a string of HTML), so a hostile title or
			description can't inject markup. A result's `url` is still followed when chosen: check it if the API isn't yours.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Focus stays in the text field; the arrows move a highlight through the results.

			| Key                   | Action                                                                |
			| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
			| Arrow Down / Arrow Up | Move the highlight through the results (stops at the ends)            |
			| Enter                 | Choose the highlighted result; with none highlighted, submit the form |
			| Escape                | Close the results; when they're already closed, clear the text        |
			| Tab                   | Leave the search                                                      |
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			The text field is an ARIA 1.2 combobox with a list of results (the WAI-ARIA authoring practices pattern).

			- `role="combobox"` with `aria-autocomplete="list"`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls` and
			  `aria-activedescendant` pointing at the highlighted result; focus stays in the text field.
			- The results are a `listbox` of `option`s; a `category` search groups them in named `group`s.
			- A hidden status message announces how many results there are, or the no-results / error message.
			- Name the search with `aria-label` on the host or a `<label for>`; the placeholder alone is not a name.
			- If the element can't render, its native fallback is a text input with the browser's suggestions (a
			  `<datalist>`) from `source`: titles only, no remote results, no events.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Set `source` as a property, not an attribute, and listen for `ui-select` or `ui-changed`.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-search name="country" source={countries} onui-select={(event) => setCountry(event.detail.result)} />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-search name="country" :source="countries" @ui-select="(event) => (country = event.detail.result)" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-search
			  name="country"
			  source={countries}
			  on-ui-choosing={(event) => setCountry(event.detail.result)}
			/>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
